        <TypeOf rdf:resource="http://tk_tdo.org/ontologies/TK_TDO#Recreational"/>
        <CostumeAttireNote>Depends on the occasion;  symbolic headbands or clan identifiers are used during festivals.</CostumeAttireNote>
        <DurationNote>Short rounds; may continue for hours depending on the enthusiasm of the players.</DurationNote>
        <HistoricalOriginNote>Traditional game played in village festivals and youth dormitory competitions.</HistoricalOriginNote>
        <MusicalInstrumentNote>played in the background to energize the crowd</MusicalInstrumentNote>
        <ParticipantsNote>Young men and women, sometimes grouped by clans or dormitories (som).</ParticipantsNote>
        <PerformedDuringNote>post-harvest celebrations and youth dormitory events</PerformedDuringNote>
        <RegionalVariationNote>Played with ropes, twisted jungle vines, or even cloth, depending on local availability.</RegionalVariationNote>
        <SymbolismNote>Represents unity, teamwork, and the balance of power between groups or clans</SymbolismNote>
        <TellsStoryOf>Friendly competition that reflects agricultural cooperation, clan unity, and festive spirit within the village.</TellsStoryOf>
        <hasSongNote>Cheering songs or rhythmic chants to encourage teams.</hasSongNote>
        <schema:description>Traditional tug-of-war game testing strength, unity, and coordination, typically played by two opposing teams pulling on a long rope.</schema:description>
        <schema:name>Khao loi</schema:name>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


