        <CostumeAttireNote>plain or sacred garments, sometimes adorned with feathers, beads, leaves, or ritual sashes</CostumeAttireNote>
        <DanceInstrumentNote>None specific; symbolic items like staffs, leaves, or sacred emblems may be held.</DanceInstrumentNote>
        <FormationNote>Solo or in small groups, encircling the sacred space or altar.</FormationNote>
        <MinCount rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</MinCount>
        <MovementNote>Slow, trance-like, with rhythmic gestures symbolizing spiritual invocation or possession.</MovementNote>
        <MusicalInstrumentNote>create a trance-inducing rhythm.</MusicalInstrumentNote>
        <ParticipantsNote>1-5 Shaman professionals performing near the altars dedicated to gods;  occasionally includes elder women in supporting roles.</ParticipantsNote>
        <PerformedDuringNote>healing rituals, agricultural blessings, and major clan rites.</PerformedDuringNote>
        <RegionalVariationNote>Minor differences in movement and chant patterns across villages or clans.</RegionalVariationNote>
        <SymbolismNote>Purification, divine guidance, protection, and ancestral reverence.</SymbolismNote>
        <TellsStoryOf>Spiritual connection, divine possession, and ancestral blessings for the community.</TellsStoryOf>
        <hasSongNote>Low-toned chants or invocations specific to the deity or spirit being addressed.</hasSongNote>
        <schema:description>A sacred ritual dance performed by spiritual specialists (Shaman) near altars to invoke communication with deities and ancestral spirits.</schema:description>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>Trance Dance</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


