        <TypeOf rdf:resource="http://tk_tdo.org/ontologies/TK_TDO#Ritual"/>
        <linkedRitual rdf:resource="http://tk_tdo.org/ontologies/TK_TDO#Sa-Ai"/>
        <CostumeAttireNote>Minimal clothing or ceremonial loincloths for agility and display.</CostumeAttireNote>
        <DurationNote>Short, event-based activity within a larger ritual day.</DurationNote>
        <FoodPracticeNote>Feasting after the event, often involving the slaughter of the mithun at a later stage.</FoodPracticeNote>
        <HistoricalOriginNote>Associated with rites of passage, feasts of merit, and public demonstrations of masculine virtue.</HistoricalOriginNote>
        <ParticipantsNote>Able-bodied men of the community, elders, and spectators including women and children.</ParticipantsNote>
        <PerformedDuringNote>Before a feast of merit, Kut festivals or during major community festivals.</PerformedDuringNote>
        <RegionalVariationNote>variations with different jumping techniques or associated rituals.</RegionalVariationNote>
        <SymbolismNote>Symbolizes valor, masculinity, and eligibility for higher social recognition; jumping over the mithun also implies control over wealth and wild strength.</SymbolismNote>
        <TellsStoryOf>A man&apos;s strength, courage, and social readiness to host a significant communal event involving mithun sacrifice.</TellsStoryOf>
        <schema:description>Siel Kal Ki Tet is a ceremonial contest where men leap over a mithun to showcase bravery, agility, and readiness to perform a feast of merit.</schema:description>
        <schema:name>Siel Kal Ki Tet</schema:name>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


