        <CostumeMen>Warrior-inspired dress</CostumeMen>
        <CostumeWomen>Bead ornaments</CostumeWomen>
        <CostumeWomen>Brightly colored woven shawls</CostumeWomen>
        <DurationNote>3 days</DurationNote>
        <HistoricalOriginNote>tied to agricultural rhythms</HistoricalOriginNote>
        <ParticipantsNote>Young boys and girls take lead roles; elders guide rituals</ParticipantsNote>
        <RegionalVariationNote>Similar names among related tribes with minor ritual differences</RegionalVariationNote>
        <SymbolismNote>Symbolic of transition from dormancy to cultivation; renewal, labor, cooperation, and harmony with the environment.</SymbolismNote>
        <TellsStoryOf>Successful clearing of forest land for jhum cultivation; youth labor, teamwork, and gratitude.</TellsStoryOf>
        <schema:description>Forest clearing festival tied to jhum cultivation;acknowledging the physical labor and teamwork;  gratitude to nature and ancestral spirits.</schema:description>
